 Happy Friday Jr.! Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 579 words, a 2-min. read. Thanks to Amy Stern for copy editing . 

 🏗️ Breaking: The Kennedy Center board decided today to move forward with a two-year closure of the sprawling performing arts venue to allow for substantial renovation work. Some outdoor programming may continue. 

 The board voted to inscribe President Trump's name on the building beneath the main signage: "Restored and Renovated by President Donald J. Trump." ( N.Y. Times gift link) 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Put it on his tab 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Suzanne Plunkett - Pool via Getty Images The East Wing is gone, a new helipad is spreading across the South Lawn, and scaffolding wraps around the White House's front columns. But the price tag for President Trump's renovations is far less obvious, Axios' Avery Lotz reports. 

 The muddled mix of funding streams includes private donors , taxpayers and, Trump has said , the president himself. 🤑 One tally finds that the president's construction projects — including his new East Wing ballroom, upgrades to Lafayette Square and more — could cost at least $927 million, The Washington Post reports (gift link) . 

 Senate Democrats are urging the Government Accountability Office to audit the ballroom project's scope and cost. The lawmakers cited a lack of transparency and "limited congressional oversight, meaningful public input, and adherence to established federal processes." (Read the letter.) 🏗️ White House spokesman Davis Ingle said in a statement that Trump "continues to implement long-overdue and necessary renovations," calling him the "Builder-in-Chief."

 Ingle said the East Wing project is "inextricably tied" to security concerns, and pointed to an alleged plot to attack this summer's UFC fight on White House grounds. Keep reading . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 🥇 Gold goes bananas 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep . Chart: Emily Peck/Axios Gold prices rose to their highest level in more than two months yesterday, hitting $4,465 an ounce, Axios' Emily Peck reports. 

 The last time gold rose 13% or more in a month was September 1999 — more than a quarter-century ago. 🖼️ The big picture: Gold's ascent signals that investors are expecting less inflation.

 When the Iran war started, they generally believed inflation would heat up, and interest rates would rise. 📈 Yes, but: Last month's Fed meeting reassured many investors that interest rates weren't about to rise — and gold started climbing.

 Yesterday's relatively benign inflation report offered even more assurance. Go deeper . 

 
 
